liburing, vendored as a static archive.

WHY, since the distro ships one:

  This box had liburing 2.15 headers and .so installed over a dpkg-managed
  liburing2 2.5 package -- dpkg owned the liburing.so.2 symlink and pointed it
  at a file dpkg did not know about.  An `apt install --reinstall liburing2`
  would have restored the symlink to 2.5 while the headers stayed 2.15.
  Vendoring pins the version at build time, so no package operation can move
  it under a built binary.

  It is also what luajit already does here, so it is one pattern rather than
  two.

WHAT IT IS NOT:

  Not a performance change.  liburing is 0.364% of this product's total CPU,
  and the three symbols that appear in a profile -- io_uring_submit,
  io_uring_wait_cqes, io_uring_wait_cqe_timeout -- are the ones that make
  syscalls, so most of that share is the kernel entry any implementation pays.
  Measured before and after, four interleaved passes: median +216 rps per
  server core, 2 of 4 positive.  Not distinguishable, and not the reason.

THE COST, STATED:

  Distro security updates for liburing no longer reach a built binary; we own
  rebuilding.  The surface is a 40 KB wrapper over three syscalls, which is why
  that trade was judged acceptable -- but it is a trade, not a free win.

REBUILD:  ./bin/build-thirdparty.sh liburing
PINNED:   liburing-2.15 (commit d41bf92)
